> For example, we can trace the pattern and then optionally convert views to reshapes, and optionally functionalize the pattern - that would likely help with the functionalization issues. Although V2 might be more work here.

@jansel did propose this for the functionalization issue and I think it is very plausible. 

Also, I just realized, register_replacement accepts a trace_fn arg: https://github.com/pytorch/pytorch/blob/c1f51cf2c4fc8259fa48bc506320118e0e907906/torch/_inductor/pattern_matcher.py#L1374 so we could just pass in a function that (1) does the tracing and then (2) converts views back into reshapes

@shaoyuyoung I think this is expected behavior. Torch.compile is generating the implementations for these operators in triton where an eager implementation has not been implemented by hand. cc @eellison 

I generated the output code for logsigmoid to confirm my hypothesis.
https://gist.github.com/mlazos/273d4e112a4c593797a936b549845a67

For workaround, we can do the view inside of the compiled region. (I checked with TORCH_LOGS=output_code)
```
import torch

@torch.library.custom_op("bug_report::foo", mutates_args=("a", "b")) # E: Untyp
def foo(a: torch.Tensor, b: torch.Tensor) -> torch.Tensor:
    a.add_(1.)
    b.add_(1.)
    return a + b

@foo.register_fake
def _(a, b): # E: Function is missing a type annotation  [no-untyped-def]
    return a.new_empty(a.shape)

@torch.compile
def bar(buf): # E: Function is missing a type annotation  [no-untyped-def]
    a = buf[1]
    b = buf[0]
    return foo(a, b)

if __name__ == "__main__":
    buf = torch.ones(2, 128, device="cpu")
    output = bar(buf)
    print(buf)
    print(output)
    print("finish")
```

